<h2 id="a">A</h2>

<h3 id="abbreviations-acronyms-and-initialisms">abbreviations, acronyms and initialisms</h3>

<span id="wg-024"></span>Write the full term the first time on a page, with the abbreviation in brackets. Do not explain ones everyone knows, such as USB, LED or TV. Put a unit symbol straight after its number. See [Use familiar, concrete words](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-010).

<h3 id="action">action</h3>

Use “action” for what Home Assistant is asked to do. Use “service” only for an older saved file. See [action](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-action).

<h3 id="active-voice">active voice</h3>

Use the active voice. Use the passive only when what happened matters more than who did it. See [Make sentences easy to follow](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-013).

<h3 id="add-on">add-on</h3>

Use “app”. See [app](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-app).

<h3 id="alarm">alarm</h3>

Use only for a real alarm that has sounded. A reminder, a warning or a low battery is not an alarm. See [Do not blame or raise false alarms](../writing-guidelines/right-tone.html#wg-046).

<h3 id="alert">alert</h3>

A message that stays until the problem is gone. A message that disappears is a toast. See [Choose urgency from evidence](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="alternative-text">alternative text</h3>

Write “alternative text” in full once, then “alt text”. Leave it empty for a decorative image. See [Write alternative text for meaningful images](../formatting-content/images.html#wg-082).

<h3 id="am-and-pm">am and pm</h3>

Use lower case with no space or full stops. Use “midnight” and “midday”, not 12am or 12pm. See [Respect number and date preferences](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-072).

<h3 id="ampersand">ampersand</h3>

Use “and”. Keep an ampersand only inside a native Home Assistant label or a name someone typed.

<h3 id="apostrophes">apostrophes</h3>

Use a curly apostrophe. Do not use one to make a plural. Never change one inside a name someone typed or inside code. See [Keep names exactly as people typed them](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-022).

<h3 id="app">app</h3>

Software that runs alongside Home Assistant OS. It is not an integration or a card. See [app](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-app).

<h3 id="area">area</h3>

A room or other space that groups devices. Use the name the household gave it. See [area](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-area).

<h3 id="assist">Assist</h3>

Capital A. Home Assistant’s voice and text assistant. Do not use another product’s name for it. See [Assist](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-assist-language-assist).

<h3 id="automation">automation</h3>

Lower case. Say whether it is saved, turned on or running, because these are three facts. See [Keep saved, enabled and running apart](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-035).

<h3 id="away">Away</h3>

Capital A when it is a label a tracker reports. It describes a phone or tracker, not a person. See [Treat presence as sensitive and uncertain](../writing-guidelines/right-tone.html#wg-066).

<h2 id="b">B</h2>

<h3 id="battery">battery</h3>

Give the level with its unit and the time it was reported. “Low” is a state, not an instruction. See [Keep units with their values](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-073).

<h3 id="bold">bold</h3>

Use for a control to select or a label on screen. Do not use for emphasis. A safety step may stress one critical word. See [Write a warning](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="brackets">brackets</h3>

Use for an abbreviation after the full term or a short note. Never put a whole sentence in brackets. Do not use square brackets in prose.

<h3 id="british-sign-language-bsl">British Sign Language (BSL)</h3>

Write in full once, then “BSL”. A written transcript is not a sign language version. Link a BSL video only when it exists. See [Describe formats accurately](../formatting-content/attachments.html#wg-064).

<h3 id="bullet-points">bullet points</h3>

See [lists](#lists).

<h3 id="button">button</h3>

Name it with a verb and its object. A button asks for something. It does not report what happened. See [Name the action and its object](../writing-guidelines/clear-titles.html#wg-030).

<h2 id="c">C</h2>

<h3 id="camera">camera</h3>

Name it by where it points. Give the time of any snapshot. Do not call a stored image live. See [Stay inside what the system can know](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-043).

<h3 id="capital-letters">capital letters and punctuation</h3>

<span id="wg-023"></span>Use sentence case for headings, labels and buttons. Use capitals for the first word, names and product names only. End a full sentence with a full stop. Do not end a heading, label or button with one.

<h3 id="card">card</h3>

A block on a dashboard. Use “card”, not “widget” or “tile”. See [card](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-dashboard-language-card).

<h3 id="card-mod">card-mod</h3>

Lower case, with a hyphen. A separate custom integration. Say why a civica card needs it.

<h3 id="celsius">Celsius</h3>

Write the symbol straight after the number. Use Fahrenheit only if the household chose it. Do not convert a reading. See [Respect number and date preferences](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-072).

<h3 id="charts">charts</h3>

See [Give charts a text equivalent](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-077).

<h3 id="click">click</h3>

Use “select”. People use touch, keyboards, voice and switches as well as a mouse.

<h3 id="clocks-changing">clocks changing</h3>

Say which hour a schedule means when the clocks change. Write “British Summer Time (BST)” and “Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)” in full once. See [Make ambiguous times explicit](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-075).

<h3 id="colon">colon</h3>

Use to introduce a list. Do not use in a heading or a label.

<h3 id="colour">colour</h3>

Use UK spelling in your own words. Keep a native label as it is. Never use colour alone to show a state. See [Do not depend on colour or position](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html#wg-081).

<h3 id="commas">commas</h3>

Use after an introductory phrase. Do not use before “and” in a list unless it prevents confusion.

<h3 id="contractions">contractions</h3>

Write “cannot” and “do not” in warnings, conditions and short instructions. Use “you’ll” and “it’s” elsewhere. Do not mix forms of one word on a screen. See [Write important conditions in full](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-014).

<h2 id="d">D</h2>

<h3 id="dashboard">dashboard</h3>

Lower case. Name it by what it is for. See [dashboard](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-dashboard-language-dashboard).

<h3 id="dates">dates</h3>

Write the day, the month in full, then the year. Do not use “2nd” or numbers only. Use “to” in a range. See [Respect number and date preferences](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-072).

<h3 id="daylight-saving-time">daylight saving time</h3>

Use “British Summer Time (BST)” and “Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)”. See [clocks changing](#clocks-changing).

<h3 id="decimal-places">decimal places</h3>

Show only the digits the sensor measures. Use a decimal point. See [Keep meaningful precision](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-071).

<h3 id="device">device</h3>

A physical product or a service that groups entities. Do not call every entity a device. See [device](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-device).

<h3 id="diagnostics">diagnostics</h3>

Say what the file contains and who can see it. Tell people to check it for private details. Give the file type and size in the link. See [Describe formats accurately](../formatting-content/attachments.html#wg-064).

<h3 id="disabled">disabled</h3>

Avoid. It can mean turned off, hidden or unavailable. Say which. Do not use about people.

<h3 id="do-not">do not</h3>

See [contractions](#contractions).

<h2 id="e">E</h2>

<h3 id="e-g">e.g.</h3>

Use “for example”, “such as” or “including”. Do not use “e.g.”, “i.e.” or “etc.”.

<h3 id="email">email</h3>

One word, lower case. Do not put an email address in a notification preview. See [Keep notifications true and private](../formatting-content/content-blocks.html#wg-045).

<h3 id="entity">entity</h3>

One reading, control or function in Home Assistant. Say “light”, “sensor” or “switch” when you can. See [entity](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-entity).

<h3 id="entity-id">entity ID</h3>

Write “entity ID”. Use code formatting when someone must copy it. Renaming an entity does not change its ID. See [Keep literal values literal](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-025).

<h3 id="error-messages">error messages</h3>

Say what happened, then what the person can do. Name only a confirmed cause. Do not blame the person. Put a message you refer to in quotation marks. See [Give a recovery that matches the cause](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-040).

<h3 id="exclamation-marks">exclamation marks</h3>

Do not use.

<h2 id="f">F</h2>

<h3 id="fahrenheit">Fahrenheit</h3>

See [Celsius](#celsius).

<h3 id="firmware">firmware</h3>

The software inside a device. Use “firmware” only if the maker does. Do not promise an update will fix a fault unless the maker says so.

<h3 id="floor">floor</h3>

A group of areas on one level. See [floor](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-floor).

<h3 id="full-stops">full stops</h3>

End every full sentence with one. Do not end a heading, label, button or short table cell with one. Do not use in abbreviations.

<h2 id="g">G</h2>

<h3 id="gateway">gateway</h3>

Use the name printed on the device or in its app. Do not use “gateway” and “hub” for one device on a page.

<h3 id="guest">guest</h3>

Do not assume who is in the home. Write “someone in your home”, not “your family”. See [Do not assume who is reading](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-060).

<h2 id="h">H</h2>

<h3 id="headings">headings</h3>

Use sentence case. Start with a verb where you can. Do not use a question or skip a level. See [Use real headings, lists and tables](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html#wg-080).

<h3 id="heating">heating</h3>

“Heating on” is a state. “Turn on heating” is an action. Do not say it is on until the boiler confirms. See [Separate state from action](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-031).

<h3 id="home-assistant">Home Assistant</h3>

Write in full the first time on a page. Do not use “HA” in text people read. Keep native labels and names people typed exactly. See [Keep native labels and translations](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-021).

<h3 id="hub">hub</h3>

Use the name printed on the device or in its app. Do not call Home Assistant a “hub”.

<h3 id="hyphens">hyphens</h3>

Use in a compound modifier only to prevent confusion. Keep native names as they are.

<h2 id="i">I</h2>

<h3 id="i-e">i.e.</h3>

See [e.g.](#e-g).

<h3 id="icons">icons</h3>

Do not use alone. Add a visible label or a text alternative. An icon that shows a state needs the state in words. See [Do not depend on colour or position](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html#wg-081).

<h3 id="integration">integration</h3>

The connection between Home Assistant and a device family or service. It is not an app. See [integration](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-integration).

<h3 id="internet">internet</h3>

Lower case. Say what stops working when the connection is lost. Do not claim a feature works without it unless the maker does.

<h3 id="ip-address">IP address</h3>

Write “IP address” in full once. Use code formatting. Do not show one on a shared screen.

<h3 id="italics">italics</h3>

Do not use. Put a document’s name in “double quotation marks”.

<h2 id="j">J</h2>

<h3 id="jargon">jargon</h3>

Use the word the household uses. Keep a technical term when it is the label on screen, and explain it once. See [Use familiar, concrete words](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-010).

<h2 id="k">K</h2>

<h3 id="kilowatts-and-kilowatt-hours">kilowatts and kilowatt-hours</h3>

Power is a rate, in kilowatts (kW) or watts (W). Energy is an amount over a period, in kilowatt-hours (kWh). Do not write “kW/h”. See [Name the measured quantity](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-070).

<h2 id="l">L</h2>

<h3 id="labels">labels</h3>

Name a control with a verb and its object. Make repeated labels different from each other. Keep the label Home Assistant shows for a native screen. See [Name the action and its object](../writing-guidelines/clear-titles.html#wg-030).

<h3 id="last-changed-last-updated-and-last-reported">last changed, last updated and last reported</h3>

Three different times. A sensor that has not changed can still be working. Give the time the source gave. See [Say which time the data covers](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-074).

<h3 id="light">light</h3>

Use “light”. Use “lamp” only for a lamp. Name it by where it is or what it is. See [light](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-light).

<h3 id="links">links</h3>

Make the link text describe the destination. A link goes somewhere. It does not run a device action. See [Describe the destination in the link text](../writing-guidelines/add-links.html#wg-083).

<h3 id="lists">lists</h3>

Use a lead-in line. Start each bullet in lower case unless it is a name. Do not end bullets with “and” or “or”. Use numbers only for steps in order. See [Use real headings, lists and tables](../formatting-content/text-formatting.html#wg-080).

<h3 id="lock">lock</h3>

“Locked” is what the lock reports. It does not prove the door is shut. See [Stay inside what the system can know](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-043).

<h2 id="m">M</h2>

<h3 id="measurements">measurements</h3>

Use numerals, metric units and no space before the unit. Never show a number without its unit. See [Keep units with their values](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-073).

<h3 id="midnight-and-midday">midnight and midday</h3>

Use “midnight” and “midday”, not 00:00, 12am, 12pm or “noon”. For a deadline, give the time and the date. See [Make ambiguous times explicit](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-075).

<h3 id="months">months</h3>

Write in full. Abbreviate only in a narrow table column.

<h3 id="motion">motion</h3>

A motion sensor reports movement, not a person. See [Treat presence as sensitive and uncertain](../writing-guidelines/right-tone.html#wg-066).

<h2 id="n">N</h2>

<h3 id="names">names</h3>

Keep the name a person gave a device exactly as typed. Do not guess a relationship from it. See [Keep names exactly as people typed them](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-022).

<h3 id="notifications">notifications</h3>

Say what was observed and when. Do not include anything a stranger should not read on a lock screen. See [Keep notifications true and private](../formatting-content/content-blocks.html#wg-045).

<h3 id="numbers">numbers</h3>

Use numerals, including 1 to 9. Use a comma in thousands. Use “to” in a range. See [Respect number and date preferences](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-072).

<h2 id="o">O</h2>

<h3 id="offline">offline</h3>

Say only if you know the connection is lost. See [Keep unavailable, unknown, stale and offline apart](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-033).

<h3 id="ok">OK</h3>

Do not use as a label. Say what will happen.

<h3 id="on-and-off">on and off</h3>

“On” and “off” are states. “Turn on” and “Turn off” are actions. Do not use “switch on” or “toggle”. See [Separate state from action](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-031).

<h2 id="p">P</h2>

<h3 id="passwords-and-tokens">passwords and tokens</h3>

Never ask for one in a field that does not need it. Never show one in a notification, a link or a log to share. See [Ask for personal information only when needed](../writing-guidelines/clear-structure.html#wg-061).

<h3 id="per-cent">per cent</h3>

Use the % symbol with a numeral and no space. Say “percentage points” when comparing two percentages.

<h3 id="person">person</h3>

Combines phones and other trackers. It is not a user account and does not prove where someone is. See [person](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-domain-person).

<h3 id="please">please</h3>

Do not use “please” or “please note”.

<h3 id="power">power</h3>

See [kilowatts and kilowatt-hours](#kilowatts-and-kilowatt-hours).

<h3 id="presence">presence</h3>

A tracker or sensor reports a device or movement, not a person. Do not show it on a shared screen unless the household chose to. See [Treat presence as sensitive and uncertain](../writing-guidelines/right-tone.html#wg-066).

<h2 id="q">Q</h2>

<h3 id="question-marks">question marks</h3>

Do not use a question as a heading. Use one in a form question that asks for a decision.

<h3 id="quotation-marks">quotation marks</h3>

Use curly double quotation marks. Use straight ones only in code. Never change quotation marks in a value someone must copy. See [Keep literal values literal](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-025).

<h2 id="r">R</h2>

<h3 id="reading">reading</h3>

What a sensor reported at a time. Say when. Do not call an old reading current. Do not show a missing reading as 0. See [Keep gaps and real zeros apart](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-076).

<h3 id="repair">repair</h3>

A Home Assistant repair explains the problem and gives numbered steps to fix it. See [Choose urgency from evidence](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="request-sent">request sent</h3>

Say “Request sent” until the device reports a result. Do not say “Done” or “Success”. See [Say a request was sent, not that it worked](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-032).

<h3 id="reset-and-restart">reset and restart</h3>

“Restart” keeps settings. “Reset” may remove them. Say what will be lost before the step. Do not use “reboot”. See [State real prerequisites and consequences](../writing-guidelines/clear-structure.html#wg-051).

<h3 id="retry">retry</h3>

Offer “Try again” only when it is safe and will not repeat the action. See [Explain pending and uncertain outcomes](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-044).

<h2 id="s">S</h2>

<h3 id="saved-sent-and-selected">saved, sent and selected</h3>

Three different facts. None proves the device did anything. See [Keep saved, enabled and running apart](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-035).

<h3 id="scene">scene</h3>

A saved set of device states requested together. It is not a timed routine. See [scene](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-scene).

<h3 id="schedule">schedule</h3>

Say what it does, on which days and by which clock. Say what happens if a device cannot be reached. See [Explain pending and uncertain outcomes](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-044).

<h3 id="select">select</h3>

Use “select” for any control. Do not use “click”, “tap”, “press” or “hit”. Use “turn on” for a toggle.

<h3 id="sensor">sensor</h3>

A device that reports a reading. A sensor reports. It does not decide. See [sensor](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-sensor).

<h3 id="settings">settings</h3>

**Settings** in bold for the Home Assistant screen. “settings” in lower case for choices in general. Do not use “config” for what a household calls settings. See [Keep native labels and translations](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-021).

<h3 id="signal-strength">signal strength</h3>

Say what it means for the person. Show a raw number only in diagnostics.

<h3 id="smart">smart</h3>

Avoid. Say what the device does. Use only inside a product name.

<h3 id="sound">sound</h3>

Never use sound alone. Give the same information in words. See [Do not assume one way to see, hear or act](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-063).

<h3 id="states">states</h3>

A state is the value Home Assistant holds for an entity. It is not necessarily fresh or what was asked for. See [Separate state from action](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-031).

<h2 id="t">T</h2>

<h3 id="temperature">temperature</h3>

Give the reading, the unit and the time. “Target” is what was set. “Current” is what the sensor reported. See [Name the measured quantity](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-070).

<h3 id="time-zones">time zones</h3>

Name the zone when a time could be misread. Use the household’s zone, not the viewer’s. See [Make ambiguous times explicit](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-075).

<h3 id="times">times</h3>

Use the household’s clock setting and keep to it on one screen. Never write “1730hrs”. Use “to” in a range. See [Respect number and date preferences](../formatting-content/charts-and-data.html#wg-072).

<h3 id="title-case">title case</h3>

Do not use. Write headings and labels in sentence case.

<h3 id="toast">toast</h3>

A short message that disappears. Use only for feedback no one needs to act on. See [Choose urgency from evidence](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="turn-on-and-turn-off">turn on and turn off</h3>

Use for lights, heating and appliances. Do not use “switch on”, “power up”, “activate” or “toggle”. Name the thing.

<h2 id="u">U</h2>

<h3 id="unavailable-and-unknown">unavailable and unknown</h3>

Neither means 0 and neither proves the device is offline. See [unavailable](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-state-records-unavailable) and [Keep unavailable, unknown, stale and offline apart](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-033).

<h3 id="undo">undo</h3>

Offer only when the system can undo the action. Say what it will do. See [Say what Cancel, Stop and Delete do](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-036).

<h3 id="units">units</h3>

See [measurements](#measurements).

<h3 id="update">update</h3>

An action and a time. Name which. Never show a page’s change date as a device’s reading. See [Say what changed and why it matters](../writing-guidelines/change-notes.html#wg-091).

<h3 id="url">URL</h3>

Write “web address”. Use code formatting only when someone must copy it. Never show one containing a token or password.

<h2 id="v">V</h2>

<h3 id="version-numbers">version numbers</h3>

Write Home Assistant versions as the maker does. Say which version a screen or label was checked against.

<h3 id="voice">voice</h3>

Use “voice” for speaking to Assist. Say what Assist heard and did. See [Assist](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-assist-language-assist).

<h2 id="w">W</h2>

<h3 id="warnings">warnings</h3>

Put a warning before the step it affects. Give the hazard, what happens and what to do. Use “Danger”, “Warning” and “Caution” only for risk to people. See [Choose urgency from evidence](../writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html#wg-042).

<h3 id="welsh">Welsh</h3>

Write English in English and Welsh in Welsh. Do not machine-translate and publish. Do not mix languages in one control. See [Preserve language and translation](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-065).

<h3 id="wifi">wifi</h3>

Lower case, no hyphen. Keep the network name exactly as typed.

<h3 id="words-to-avoid">words to avoid</h3>

Do not use “simply”, “just”, “easy”, “obviously” or “please”. See [Use familiar, concrete words](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-010).

<h2 id="y">Y</h2>

<h3 id="you-and-your">you and your</h3>

Use “you” and “your”. Do not use “the user”. Use “we” only when it is clear who “we” is. See [Do not assume who is reading](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-060).

<h2 id="z">Z</h2>

<h3 id="z-wave">Z-Wave</h3>

Capital Z and W, with a hyphen.

<h3 id="zigbee">Zigbee</h3>

Capital Z. Do not write “ZigBee” or “zigbee”.

<h3 id="zone">zone</h3>

A place on a map for location features. It is not a room. See [zone](technical-a-to-z.html#ht-zone).

